.TH SDATA 2
.SH NAME
sdata \- manipulate a shared data segment
.SH SYNOPSIS
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
.B "sdata(arg, reg, offset)"
.B "char *arg;"
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
.B "sdata(arg, 0, offset)"
.B "char *arg;"
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B sdata
manipulates a shared data segment.
On the PDP-11, the segment
is placed in the 8kb user data segment indicated by argument
.IR reg .
On
the 8086, the extra memory segment is used.
.PP
The operation is given by
.IR arg :
.TP
.I filename
If
.I arg
is a file name (null terminated string), then a ``named''
segment is opened; that file is windowed into the shared segment.
If this is the first process to call up the file, then the file is first
read into memory; if the file has already been
.BR sdata 'd
by another
process, then it is hooked up without further I/O.
The window
will be placed into the file at an initial offset given by
.IR offset \(mu64
bytes (PDP-11) or
.IR offset \(mu512
bytes (8086).
.sp
The size of the shared data segment is given by the length of the
file, rounded up to the next 64 (PDP-11) or 512 (8086) byte
boundary.
.TP
.B "(char *) 0"
The window offset into the previously hooked-to segment is
changed to
.IR offset \(mu64
(PDP-11) or
.IR offset \(mu512
(8086) bytes.
This
allows the user to move his window to any location in the segment.
.TP
.B "(char *) 1"
An ``unnamed'' segment is opened.
This segment is not associated
with any disk file, and can not be shared by multiple
processes; it merely allows an individual process to hook into an
extra memory area.
.I offset
is the length of the segment, in 64
(PDP-11) or 512 (8086) byte units.
.TP
.B "(char *) 2"
(reserved for future use)
.TP
.B "(char *) 3"
The previously opened segment (named or unnamed) is closed,
and memory is unmapped for the calling process.
Closing a
named segment does not affect any other processes hooked to the
same segment.
When a named segment is no longer held open
by any processes, it is dropped from memory unless the associated
file has the `sticky bit' set (mode 01000 \- see
.BR chmod (2)).
In this case the segment remains intact forever.
.PP
The user is responsible for making sure that the memory mapped for the
shared data segment is not otherwise used by his program.
The
.B sdata
call returns an error if it is.
See
.BR phys (2).
.PP
Only one shared data segment per process can be hooked to at a time.
If
several processes may be simultaneously writing to the same area at once,
you will probably wish to use semaphores (see
.BR semset (2))
to prevent
conflicts.
.PP
.B "PDP-11 NOTES"
.br
After hooking to a shared data segment, PDP-11 programs may access it
as part of their normal user memory.
On the PDP-11, the top 8kb of
virtual memory (register 7) is always reserved for the program stack, and
should never be used; however, register 6 will be available unless the program
is very large, or unless it has been previously allocated by
.BR phys (2)
(see also note below concerning
.BR phys ).
The
.BR size (1)
command can be used
to determine the amount of space used by your program.
.PP
All free memory pages can be mapped into the same shared file (presumably
with different offsets) by opening the shared file, and then setting
.I arg
to zero and giving different values of
.I reg
on subsequent
.B sdata
calls.
.PP
The mapping of physical memory (with
.BR phys (2))
to another register may
be lost when a shared segment is opened; to be safe, if
.B phys
calls are
needed they should be done after opening the shared segment.
.PP
.B "8086 NOTES"
.br
On the 8086, the
.B getes
and
.B putes
calls may be used to read and write to
the shared segment once it is hooked to.
See
.BR getes (3).
.SH "SEE ALSO"
phys(2), semset(2), getes(3)
.SH DIAGNOSTICS
On error \-1 is returned.
.PP
Shared data segments is a feature of VENIX which is not portable to
standard UNIX.
.SH ASSEMBLER
(sdata = 49.)
.TP
PDP-11:
.nf
sys sdata; file; reg; offset
.fi
.TP
8086:
.nf
BX = 49
AX = file
DX = ?
CX = offset
int 0Xf1
.fi
.sp
